    "detail": "<h3>First appearance of Cetacea<\/h3> <p>Following the extinction at the end of the Cretaceous, many ecological niches remained vacant. However, in the freshwater streams of early Eocene Indo-Pakistan a lineage of small artiodactyls began to adapt to aquatic life. These animals were the first cetaceans and would eventually become completely aquatic, spread around the globe's oceans, and evolve into the largest animals that have ever existed.<br><br> These first cetaceans looked dramatically different from their modern counterparts, more closely resembling a dog than a dolphin. They still had fur, hindlimbs, and nostrils at the end of their snout; all things that would change as they became better adapted to aquatic life. Despite their terrestrial appearance, they also possessed dense bones and adaptations for hearing in water, whilst isotopic analyses also indicate they were feeding in freshwater.<br><br>Contributed by: Travis Park<\/p> <p class=\"etag\">Eon: Phanerozoic · Era: Cenozoic · Period: Palaeogene<\/p>",
