    "detail": "<h3>Pliocene marine megafauna extinction<\/h3> <p>Sharks have shown incredible resilience over their evolutionary history. Still, their diversity has waxed and waned over time. In addition to the loss of diversity during the Cretaceous/Paleogene (K/Pg) mass extinction, another period of decline occurred near the end of the Pliocene, when global temperatures cooled and sea level fell. Many marine megafauna were affected, including marine mammals, seabirds, sea turtles, and sharks, along with other taxa near the base of the food web. For example, this marked the final chapter in the story of megalodon (Otodus megalodon), one of the largest predators to have ever lived, which went extinct around 2.6 million years ago.<br><br>Contributed by: Erin Dillon<\/p> <p class=\"etag\">Eon: Phanerozoic · Era: Cenozoic · Period: Pliocene<\/p>",
